{#
  Docker daemon policy for a host whose only containers are playground runs.

  Every run is started with --network=none, so the daemon needs no container
  networking at all. Turning the default bridge off and leaving the firewall
  alone is what keeps Docker out of nftables: with "iptables" false there are no
  Docker rules for templates/nftables.conf.j2 to clobber, and with "bridge" none
  there is no docker0 interface and nothing to forward. That is a stronger
  guarantee than ordering the two services carefully, and it reinforces the
  `chain forward { policy drop; }` the host firewall already applies.

  The one thing that does need the network is building the sandbox image, which
  downloads a pinned compiler tarball. deploy/playground/build-image.sh passes
  --network=host for exactly that reason.

  userns-remap is deliberately absent: the broker mounts a 0700 job directory
  into the container and passes --user with the host uid that owns it, which a
  remapped user namespace would shift out from under the mount.
#}
{
  "bridge": "none",
  "iptables": false,
  "ip6tables": false,
  "live-restore": true,
  "no-new-privileges": true,
  "log-driver": "json-file",
  "log-opts": {
    "max-size": "10m",
    "max-file": "3"
  },
  "default-ulimits": {
    "nofile": {
      "Name": "nofile",
      "Hard": {{ playground_container_nofile | int }},
      "Soft": {{ playground_container_nofile | int }}
    }
  }
}
